## Background and context

The Conflict & Humanitarian Data Centre (CHDC) is INSO’s incident database platform used across 22 countries. It helps humanitarian organisations and analysts monitor conflict trends, assess threats to aid workers, and produce timely, context-specific analysis.

CHDC had grown in value and scale but had serious usability issues. The original design was created around 2016–2017. However due to different challenges, the first version wasn’t completed until five years later. By the time it launched, both the UI and UX were somewhat outdated, misaligned with modern expectations, and inconsistent with INSO’s updated brand style.

I wasn’t officially tasked with a redesign. The initiative began when I created a UI draft inspired by INSO’s new brand guide (a separate project I led with the communications team). That draft gained traction, and the redesign quickly evolved into a broader UX improvement project involving:

- UI revamp using the new INSO brand style
- Improved navigation and readability
- Enhancements to critical flows like filtering and incident insertion
- New onboarding via in-app video tutorials
- Better visibility of incident data and more specifically impact data

## Understanding the problem

Although we had limited analytics before the redesign, Microsoft Clarity gave us useful heatmaps showing the most used elements on CHDC’s front page, the data table.

**Commonly clicked elements included:**

- Incident filters (3,5,6 on the visual below) – location, status, date/time
- Filter reset (4)
- Export (12)
- Add incident (1)
- Left sidebar (13) – Reset selected incident and go back to incidents overview
- Data table refresh (7)

![Heatmap of frequently used/clicked features/areas. (tracked and generated using MS Clarity)](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-old-popular-ui-sections.jpg)

*Heatmap of frequently used/clicked features/areas. (tracked and generated using MS Clarity)*

**Using MS Clarity session recordings, we could also observe users when facing challenges in accomplishing certain tasks and we could also list out a number of rarely used or confusing features. This included:**

1. Overview sidebar took up too much space and wasn’t frequently consulted.
2. Users would sometimes click by mistake on the wrong status indicator to change the status of an incident. This happened mostly when there was a stack of incidents with similar state colour.
3. Syncing data in the table available through the user dropdown menu.
4. Wireless icon representing a mode called “offline” which wasn’t used as expected. The necessity of this feature wasn’t researched prior to its implementation.
5. Help section available only through the user dropdown menu.
6. About CHDC available only through the user dropdown menu.
7. Alert archive – Not relevant anymore since the release of the INSO Alert Mobile App (which is a separate project showcased here).
8. Full screen toggle.

![User challenges & rarely used features](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-former-improv-focus-points.jpg)

*User challenges & rarely used features*

**Some of the most visible problems were not captured in analytics, but emerged through internal feedback. For example:**

- New users often didn’t know how to use CHDC
- Navigation was confusing and often ignored
- Users struggled to locate key impact data

These helped shape our UX direction.

