### **Table layout improvements**

The former table design focused on fitting more rows per screen to reduce vertical scrolling, but this made the interface feel dense and overwhelming — especially for less tech-savvy users. Many struggled with the unintuitive horizontal scrollbar and often preferred exporting the data to view it more comfortably.

![Data table – former design](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-former-incident-table-layout.jpg)

*Data table – former design*

In **the redesigned version**, we introduced greater spacing between rows and columns, improved readability with clearer contrast and typography, and added a fixed horizontal scroll button to make navigation easier. These changes made the interface feel more approachable, helping users focus on digesting the content of each incident row.

![Data table – updated design](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-new-incident-table-layout.jpg)

*Data table – updated design*

### Incident status redesign

Previously, statuses like “Incomplete” or “Verified” were shown in colored blocks that **felt too cramped** and **took more horizontal space** from the table than it needed. We switched to compact colored indicators with padding around them, which improved legibility and made it easier to visually group incidents by status.

![](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-incident-status-comparison.png)

### Programme selection clarity

In the old version, selected country programmes were shown in multiple tags, often taking up too much space. We simplified this by showing a single selected programme by name, or a count of selected programmes when multiple are chosen — using a standard dropdown menu for clarity.

![](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-incident-country-indications-comparison.png)

### Reworking the incidents overview

The incidents overview used to be a fixed sidebar, which took up space even when not needed. Now, it’s accessible via a button and opens as a modal overlay. This lets users view more detailed incident summaries without cluttering the main interface.

**Former design**

![Incident overview – Former design (taking up sidebar space)](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-former-incident-overview.jpg)

*Incident overview – Former design (taking up sidebar space)*

**Redesign**

![Incidents Overview Redesign – Accessible through a button available on top of the incident table.](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-new-incident-overview-02.jpg)

*Incidents Overview Redesign – Accessible through a button available on top of the incident table.*![](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-logo-section-comparison.png)

### **Clearer branding**

Instead of the acronym-only logo, we displayed the full name — “Conflict & Humanitarian Data Centre” alongside the logo in the header section (as shown below). This made it easier for new users to recognise the platform and remember what it stands for.

**Brand style and typography**

The entire interface was adapted to the new INSO brand system, using official colours, updated iconography, and the ASAP typeface. The goal was to not only unify CHDC with other INSO platforms but also improve overall readability and visual hierarchy.

This wasn’t just a visual update and these changes were aimed at reducing cognitive load, improving information clarity, and supporting faster decision-making.

### Redesigning the data visualisation section

The previous “Charts” section was restructured to enhance usability, improve accessibility, and better align with INSO’s visual identity. The focus was on simplifying the creation of meaningful visuals while supporting users with clearer defaults and helpful resources.

Key changes:

- Renamed “Charts” to “Data visualisations” for better reflect the broader scope including maps
- Introduced a new “Visualisations by INSO” section with most-requested charts available by default.

![](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-visualisation-toolbar-comparison.png)

- Added preset visualisation options focused on key impact types, such as victims and property.

![Former pie chart design](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-former-pie-chart-page.jpg)

*Former pie chart design*
![Pie chart design refresh](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-new-pie-chart-page.jpg)

*Pie chart design refresh*

### Documentation & Introductory tutorials

To improve onboarding and reduce user friction, we introduced an online CHDC documentation along with short video tutorials. These walk users through key sections of the platform and explain how to use major features like filtering, data entry, and visualisations. Tutorials are suggested to new users upon login and always accessible via the Help section, making support available at any stage of the user journey.

![](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-documentation.jpg)

## Explorative concept: AI assistant

To explore whether AI could reduce navigation friction, I prototyped a conversational assistant using Figma Make with the idea of allowing users to query data and generate visualisations through natural language.

*User filters data using natural language: “Show me the humanitarian incidents only.”*

*User generates a chart through a prompt(in Arabic): “Create a chart showing humanitarian workers affected by incidents in Iraq.”*

## Outcomes and impact

Usage data from **March 2025** onward shows that CHDC moved beyond initial discovery and into sustained use. Adoption continued steadily over time, with a noticeable uplift following the **CHDC launch webinars** in **April 2025**.

In parallel, the number of **monthly active users** also **increased**, indicating that users were not only discovering the platform but returning to it regularly.

Together, these trends suggest that CHDC became progressively more embedded in users’ workflows, and that pairing product improvements with targeted onboarding and enablement had a positive impact on engagement.

![User adoption continued steadily, with a clear uplift following the CHDC launch webinars in April 2025, suggesting enablement played a role in driving uptake.](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-user-adoption-march-2025.jpg)

*User adoption continued steadily, with a clear uplift following the CHDC launch webinars in April 2025, suggesting enablement played a role in driving uptake.*
![Monthly active usage increased after April, indicating CHDC was not only discovered but increasingly returned to and used over time.](../../assets/projects/conflict-and-humanitarian-data-centre/chdc-monthly-active-users.jpg)

*Monthly active usage increased after April, indicating CHDC was not only discovered but increasingly returned to and used over time.*

In addition to insights gathered through surveys and webinars, users consistently highlighted the following improvements:

- Faster onboarding for new users
- Less confusion around filtering and navigation
- Easier export and incident insertion
- Improved visual clarity of incident status
- Clearer distinction between humanitarian and property impact

Some improvements are harder to quantify, but have enhanced the platform’s usability for both daily tasks and strategic reporting.

## Reflection and takeaways

The CHDC redesign wasn’t commissioned. It started as a UI draft that filled a real gap. That shaped how the project grew and what we were able to achieve within its constraints.

**Key lessons**:

- Even small interface tweaks (like colour contrast, spacing or filters) can radically improve comprehension
- Working closely with analysts and field staff keeps designs grounded
- Embedding brand identity in UI strengthens product trust

In future iterations, I’d push for more user testing earlier in the process and ideally expand tutorials into contextual help elements.
